 of the affair she wished to settle , he told her , that being extremely unwilling to discuss a matter of business with a lady , and apprehensive of giving offence to one for whom he and his dear Mrs. Crofts had so sincere a regard , he had determined to leave all the concerns yet between them to his attorney ; a man of strict honour and probity , to whom he would give her a direction , and to whom it would be better for her attorney to apply , than that they should themselves enter on a topic whereon it was probable they might differ . Mrs. Stafford , vexed at his dissimulation and finesse , again pressed him to come to a conclusion without the interference of lawyers . But he again repeated the set speech he had formed on the occasion ; and then addressing himself to Emmeline , asked smilingly , and affecting an interest in her welfare , “
